WEBVTT

1
00:00:15.093 --> 00:00:18.275
burst into Grandpa's study, blowing on a paper noisemaker.

2
00:00:18.295 --> 00:00:24.177
"Grandpa, will you come downstairs to watch the countdown with us?"

3
00:00:25.277 --> 00:00:29.779
It was New Year's Eve, and Grandpa had been in his study since after dinner.

4
00:00:29.799 --> 00:00:31.460
"Of course!

5
00:00:32.564 --> 00:00:33.845
Is it that time already?"

6
00:00:34.205 --> 00:00:34.966
Grandpa said.

7
00:00:34.986 --> 00:00:38.568
"Yep. It's almost 2011!"

8
00:00:39.569 --> 00:00:41.850
Laurie looked at the calendar hanging on the wall.

9
00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:44.372
"You need a new calendar!"

10
00:00:44.392 --> 00:00:52.818
"I always find it amazing that humans have been on Earth for well over ten thousand years, but

11
00:00:52.898 --> 00:00:55.860
our calendar only says 2011!"

12
00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:57.061
Grandpa said.

13
00:00:57.081 --> 00:00:59.523
"What year is it really?"

14
00:00:59.883 --> 00:01:01.404
Laurie asked, confused.

15
00:01:02.236 --> 00:01:04.778
"It depends on when you start your calendar.

16
00:01:05.999 --> 00:01:10.682
Most of the world uses a calendar that begins with the birth of Jesus Christ.

17
00:01:13.204 --> 00:01:18.507
Every year before that is marked with 'BC,' which stands for before Christ.

18
00:01:20.569 --> 00:01:27.873
Every year after that is marked by 'AD,' which stands for the Latin words anno Domini, meaning in

19
00:01:27.913 --> 00:01:29.154
the year of the Lord.

20
00:01:30.255 --> 00:01:35.882
Before Jesus, most calendars were based on the cycles of the moon or the sun, or both.

21
00:01:37.023 --> 00:01:42.906
It was also common to count years based on the start of a leader's or dynasty's rule.

22
00:01:43.967 --> 00:01:46.008
But Jesus changed the world.

23
00:01:47.168 --> 00:01:52.471
In the sixth century, a monk created a calendar that began with the birth of Jesus."

24
00:01:54.312 --> 00:01:58.194
"So the birth of one man changed the way humans record time?"

25
00:01:58.595 --> 00:01:59.355
Laurie asked.

26
00:02:00.256 --> 00:02:02.157
"Yes. And not only that.

27
00:02:03.237 --> 00:02:09.281
His life and death also gave rise to the largest religion in the world—Christianity."

28
00:02:10.401 --> 00:02:14.303
"I only know a little about Jesus from the stories around Christmas time."

29
00:02:15.404 --> 00:02:16.825
Laurie glanced at the clock.

30
00:02:17.925 --> 00:02:20.027
It was 11:46 p.m.

31
00:02:20.047 --> 00:02:22.988
"What was so special about his life?"

32
00:02:24.529 --> 00:02:29.492
"I'll tell you as much as I can—but quickly, because I know Mom and Dad are waiting for

33
00:02:29.572 --> 00:02:35.948
us. Jesus was born in Bethlehem, a Jewish city that was part of the Roman Empire.

34
00:02:37.029 --> 00:02:43.054
He was the son of a carpenter named Joseph and a woman named Mary, who raised him in

35
00:02:43.074 --> 00:02:44.075
the Jewish tradition.

36
00:02:45.216 --> 00:02:50.100
Many people believed he was the leader who would free the Jews from Roman rule.

37
00:02:51.041 --> 00:02:53.363
Others thought he was the son of God.

38
00:02:54.964 --> 00:02:59.208
Anyway, Jesus studied Judaism carefully and became a preacher.

39
00:03:00.332 --> 00:03:03.795
He preached about love, heaven, and the forgiveness of sins.

40
00:03:04.956 --> 00:03:08.578
He wanted to tell people everywhere that God loved them.

41
00:03:10.199 --> 00:03:17.665
Now, although Jesus worshipped the same god as the Jews, his teachings strayed from traditional Jewish beliefs.

42
00:03:19.947 --> 00:03:23.109
So some Jews accepted him, but many others did not.

43
00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:31.113
And the Romans didn't bother him, because they generally allowed their subjects to follow different religions as long

44
00:03:31.173 --> 00:03:32.594
as no one harmed the state.

45
00:03:33.674 --> 00:03:40.556
As Jesus gained more and more followers, however, many Jews and Romans began to worry about his power.

46
00:03:41.716 --> 00:03:47.178
When Jesus was just 33 years old, a Roman official ordered him to be killed.

47
00:03:49.758 --> 00:03:57.193
But even after his death, Jesus' followers kept believing in what he had said, and Christianity arose.

48
00:03:58.433 --> 00:04:04.636
At first this religion was practiced by only a small number of people, and not many others understood

49
00:04:04.656 --> 00:04:13.601
it. But as Jesus' followers journeyed to tell others about their new faith, Christianity spread throughout the Mediterranean

50
00:04:13.661 --> 00:04:20.284
world. These early Christians weren't treated very well in the Roman Empire.

51
00:04:21.444 --> 00:04:26.248
Roman emperors often held ceremonies related to Roman gods and goddesses.

52
00:04:27.329 --> 00:04:29.670
They wanted all their people to participate.

53
00:04:30.731 --> 00:04:36.275
But Christians believed in a single god and wouldn't take part in anything related to Roman gods.

54
00:04:37.436 --> 00:04:43.961
So some Roman leaders saw Christians as a threat because they obeyed their faith rather than the Roman

55
00:04:44.021 --> 00:04:47.904
emperor. These Romans persecuted Christians.

56
00:04:49.472 --> 00:04:54.636
But Christianity continued to gain popularity because it gave people hope.

57
00:04:55.817 --> 00:05:00.480
Christians spoke of life after death, when people would live in the kingdom of God.

58
00:05:02.381 --> 00:05:05.844
They also said that all people were equal before God.

59
00:05:06.544 --> 00:05:10.227
This included everyone—from emperors to commoners."

60
00:05:10.247 --> 00:05:16.371
"So I guess common people liked the religion, but the emperors didn't?"

61
00:05:16.771 --> 00:05:17.532
Laurie asked.

62
00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:20.981
"Right. Not at first anyway.

63
00:05:22.021 --> 00:05:30.865
But in 312 AD, the Roman emperor Constantine converted to Christianity, and persecution of the Christians ended.

64
00:05:32.006 --> 00:05:41.169
In 380 Emperor Theodosius made Christianity the empire's official religion, creating the world's first Christian empire.

65
00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:47.032
Gradually Christians began settling in large communities and building churches.

66
00:05:48.256 --> 00:05:52.078
Bishops, who were the heads of churches, became more powerful.

67
00:05:53.138 --> 00:05:58.800
In big cities, such as Rome, their authority extended from religion into politics.

68
00:06:00.061 --> 00:06:06.244
Once Christianity gained the approval of Roman emperors, it grew and grew within the empire.

69
00:06:07.364 --> 00:06:09.345
Churches were built using state money.

70
00:06:10.365 --> 00:06:16.768
Courts run by churches appeared, and they soon held more power than the Roman government over religious issues.

71
00:06:17.908 --> 00:06:22.489
The bishops of Rome, known as popes, became especially influential.

72
00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:26.090
They claimed authority over all other churches.

73
00:06:27.251 --> 00:06:33.372
At the same time, the Roman Empire was being attacked by nomadic troops from Europe and Asia.

74
00:06:34.893 --> 00:06:41.694
As the state grew weaker and Christianity grew stronger, people found more security with the church than they

75
00:06:41.734 --> 00:06:42.515
did with the state.

76
00:06:43.775 --> 00:06:47.336
Soon Christianity spread beyond the Roman Empire.

77
00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:56.225
Part of Jesus' message was that God was universal, so Christians believed they should spread Jesus' teachings throughout

78
00:06:56.265 --> 00:06:56.765
the world.

79
00:06:57.946 --> 00:07:04.169
Roman roads and access to the Mediterranean Sea made it easy for Christians to go on missions.

80
00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:09.833
Christians moved north throughout Europe and converted the people there.

81
00:07:10.773 --> 00:07:18.013
By the time the Roman Empire reached its last days around 500 AD, Christianity had become the dominant

82
00:07:18.073 --> 00:07:18.953
religion in Europe.

83
00:07:20.393 --> 00:07:25.475
And that brings us to the eve of the Middle Ages, Laurie, when the Christian church and the

84
00:07:25.535 --> 00:07:29.476
pope became the supreme authority on nearly everything."

85
00:07:30.516 --> 00:07:32.157
"The eve of the Middle Ages?

86
00:07:33.197 --> 00:07:39.599
Grandpa, it's the eve of 2011 and here we are sitting and talking about stuff that happened over

87
00:07:39.619 --> 00:07:40.399
1,500 years ago!

88
00:07:43.976 --> 00:07:47.138
Let's go downstairs and celebrate what's happening now!"

89
00:07:48.318 --> 00:07:49.999
Laurie grabbed Grandpa's hand.

90
00:07:50.019 --> 00:07:55.122
"Okay. You have a point," Grandpa said, standing up.

91
00:07:56.243 --> 00:07:57.503
"I should stop rambling."

92
00:08:00.125 --> 00:08:02.926
"Happy 2011, anno Domini!"

93
00:08:03.286 --> 00:08:06.008
Laurie cried as they walked out of the study together.
